ectacle meant to shock an  awe  the corpse of an alleged criminal  hanging  from a crane  pinned to the man is a note that reads  this is the punishment for kidnapping  it's a signal to criminals says this  taliban commander that they are not safe  and a foreboding sign to the world that  brutal punishments are back in  afghanistan  executions and amputations will be  restored  with one caveat not supported by this  display  that they won't take place in public  that's according to mola tarabi a senior  taliban figure he told the associated  press that no one will tell us what our  laws should be we will follow islam and  we will make our laws on the quran  in the capital kabul crime was a huge  problem before the recent taliban  takeover  the collapsing war economy led to a wave  of robberies and drug related killings  many residents have said off-camera that  they fear the taliban's ruthlessness  but on camera it's another story  it's a good thing others will learn a  lesson from it  i believe that what they're doing about  catching thieves and punishing them is  very good for the law and order  situation  but they should not do this without some  legal recourse  but that's an expectation the taliban  appear nowhere close to respecting  despite assurances  to the contrary  joining us is dw's nick connolly in  kabul nick i suppose it's too much from  the taliban to expect that this kind of  punishment they're warning about  executions amputations and hangings take  place with any due process or court  proceedings i mean has the country's  courts been completely dismantled  melissa this is a country in flux the  old system has gone or at least ground  to halt and the new system hasn't been  introduced and it's very patchy across  the country so those kind of killings  that you saw in that report have been  reported from places like herrera mazar  sharif less so here in kabul um i think  the taliban is are just at a point now  of trying to somehow unify their control